<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apia is the capital and largest town of Samoa, on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>. It grew in the 19th century as the main harbour and trading post of the islands, where German, British and American interests competed, and in March 1889 a hurricane wrecked the German and American warships gathered there. It was the seat of German Samoa from 1900 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> administration of Western Samoa from 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apia.jpg" alt="Apia" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samoa_-_Apia_Govt_buildings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Apia stands on a natural harbour at the mouth of the Vaisigano River, on the central north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>, the second-largest island of Samoa. It lies on a narrow coastal plain, with Mount Vaea, 472 metres high and the burial place of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-louis-stevenson">Robert Louis Stevenson</a>, directly to the south. The climate is tropical rainforest, with an average temperature of 26 °C and roughly 3,000 millimetres of rain a year, the wettest months being December to March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Apia was originally a small village, with a population of 304 in 1800, from which the later capital took its name. The village still exists inside the modern urban area, with its own matai (leaders) and fa'alupega (genealogy and customary greetings). The modern city was founded in the 1850s, and the high chief Seumanutafa Pogai held his title there until his death in 1898.</p><p>On 15 March 1889 seven warships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> refused to leave the harbour as a typhoon approached, each unwilling to be the first to move. All were sunk or damaged beyond repair except the British cruiser Calliope, which steamed out at about a mile an hour and rode out the storm. Nearly 200 American and German sailors died.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> ruled the western islands as German Samoa from 1900 to 1914, with Apia as capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> occupied them in August 1914 and governed them from 1920 until independence in 1962, first under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> and then, after 1945, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau movement</a> organised non-violent protests in Apia's streets; on 28 December 1929, called Black Saturday, the New Zealand constabulary killed the paramount chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a> during a peaceful Mau gathering. From 1941 to 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> ran Naval Base Upolu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Apia is the capital and largest city of Samoa and has been its official capital since 1959. The urban area had 35,974 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and consists of about 45 traditional and freehold villages, with no single city administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulinuu">Mulinuʻu</a>, the old ceremonial capital at its western end, holds the Parliament House. Stevenson's home at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vailima">Vailima</a> is now a museum. The Bahá’í House of Worship for the Pacific, opened in 1984, stands in the city, and Apia Harbour is the country's busiest port.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An influenza epidemic then killed some 7,500 Samoans, around 22 per cent of the population, after the ship SS Talune was allowed to dock at <b>Apia</b> with its crew and passengers obviously sick.</blockquote><a href="/movement/mau-movement">Mau movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Tripartite Convention of 1899 partitioned the Kingdom of Samoa, Germany received the western islands of Upolu and Savaiʻi, and the colony was proclaimed at <b>Apia</b> on 1 March 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>As the German firm Godeffroy developed plantations near <b>Apia</b>, Samoan chiefs formed a central government at Mulinuʻu in 1873, with a council of chiefs and an assembly.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the League of Nations granted New Zealand a mandate over the territory, governed from 1920 by a civil administrator at <b>Apia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
